-2. Valuation Engine Selection:\nPrimary: Engine 2 \u2014 Cycle-Adjusted Valuation (normalized earnings through the cycle; mid-cycle EBITDA path anchored on management's 2030 framework)\nCross-check: Engine 1 \u2014 Traditional DCF (FCFF) with explicit per-year forecast; terminal value sensitivity shown.\nBlend weight: Engine 2 \u00d7 60% + Engine 1 \u00d7 40% [Assumption \u2014 reflecting the ~50-60% transition status from Layer 0-3].\n\nStep 0-3. Transition Status: Cyclical \u2192 Quality at ~50-60% of criteria (recent ROIC inflection, 2 consecutive strong years, but only one cycle tested). Transition-period rule applied: Conviction \u22120.5 steps; blended engine used.\n\nStep 0-4. Why Engine 2? A DCF-only that anchors solely on the current mid-cycle EBITDA would overpay at the peak or undervalue the trough. Engine 2 with explicit mid-cycle normalized margins (6.5\u20137.5% EBITDA) and scenario-tested cycles captures both. Error if alternative engine used: Engine 4 (Expectation-Driven) would wrongly treat CHEF's growth as optionality rather than as an operational plan; Engine 3 (Product-Platform) would invent a platform premium CHEF does not have.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 1. MACRO CONTEXT ANALYSIS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 1-1. Macro Variables:\n- 10-Year Treasury: 4.71% as of 2026-08-21, +0.44pp YoY [Actual \u2014 Trading Economics]\n- Fed funds: 3.50\u20133.75%, held for a fifth consecutive meeting in July 2026; market prices ~1/3 probability of a hike [Actual \u2014 Trading Economics]\n- Real rates: 10Y nominal 4.71% minus core CPI ~3.0\u20133.4% \u2192 real \u2248 1.3\u20131.7% [Estimate]\n- DXY: \u2248 99.0, weakest in 3 months, softening with Fed-tightening bets scaled back [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics/Bloomberg]\n- Inflation: headline CPI 3.4% YoY (July 2026), down from 3.5% [Actual \u2014 BLS via TradingEconomics]\n- Credit spreads: not held in pack; not retrieved before budget exhausted [Unconfirmed]\n- Economic cycle: late expansion \u2014 hospitality/leisure demand still positive, but yield curve (10y\u20133m: +0.83pp, Aug 24) is near inversion levels; recession risk is material [Estimate]\n- Liquidity: global liquidity moderately tightening with the Fed on hold vs QT; not an easing regime [Assumption]\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Probability Distribution (must sum to 100%) [Estimate \u2014 synthesized from above]:\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 35%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 25%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 20%\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 20%\n\nStep 1-3. CHEF Macro Sensitivity (\u22123 to +3 on each axis):\n- Interest rate sensitivity: +2 (variable-rate term loan; consumer discretionary leverage; 2028 converts)\n- Dollar sensitivity: +1 (weak dollar helps specialty import margins, but much of COGS is domestic)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (ABL facility availability matters in a downturn)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: +3 (restaurant/hotel spending is discretionary and cyclical)\n- Commodity sensitivity: +2 (food inflation 4.9% at CHEF in Q2'26 supports revenue but squeezes margins when volatile)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment: Regime-weighted macro impact \u2248 \u22120.8 \u2192 Headwind [Estimate].\nRationale: Reflation risks (higher-for-longer rates) hurt CHEF's consumer demand and interest cost; recession risks depress case volumes; stagflation squeezes margins. Only a Goldilocks outcome is strongly positive. Conviction \u22120.5; split-entry strategy if any entry were otherwise justified.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates [Estimate \u2014 industry/framework base rates]:\n- 5-year survival probability for an established food distributor with 12 years of post-IPO history: >95% (Sysco ~55 years; US Foods >160 years). Base rate says survival is not the risk; return on capital is.\n- Probability of a food distributor with FY202-22 sub-WACC ROIC converting to >WACC ROIC within 3 years: ~35\u201345%.\n- M&A-driven grower achieving its 5-year EBITDA-margin target (7.5\u20138%): ~20\u201330% for similar distribution tuck-in consolidators.\n- Cyclical peak-to-40%-drawdown within 3 years for a hospitality-facing name: ~30\u201340% (reference: CHEF itself fell 30% revenue / \u2212$103M EBIT in 2020).\n- Large-convert/leverage-driven dilution event (2028 converts) cutting EPS growth to below 5% in the year of conversion: ~25\u201330% [Estimate]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation Check: My success probability for CHEF hitting its 2030 plan (\u224850% chance of landing in the 7.5\u20138% EBITDA margin band) is at the high end of the 20\u201330% base rate, close to 1.5x+ [Estimate]. Company-specific justifications for the deviation:\n1. Beat-and-raise cadence in 2025\u201326: FY2025 adjusted EBITDA \u2248 $258M [Arithmetic \u2014 Q4 $80.3M + Q3 $65.1M + Q2 $65.4M + Q1 $47.5M] exceeded the original guidance range ($276\u2013286M was the Q4'25 raise for a different year? \u2014 actually the Q4'25 release stated FY2025 adjusted EBITDA of $276\u2013286M? \u2014 see note: menafn stated Q4'25 release shows FY2025 guidance of $276\u2013286M; actual FY2025 was $258M by sum, so there is a discrepancy. I flag this as a data conflict [Unconfirmed \u2014 I could not resolve whether the $276\u2013286M was the FY2025 initial guide, a raise, or a typo]. Regardless, the Q1'26 and Q2'26 beats (+38% and +35% adj EBITDA YoY) were each above consensus [Estimate]).\n2. Visibility through unique placements (+7.2% YoY) and organic case growth (+6.0%) is unusually high for distribution [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call].\n3. The 2030 targets are not aggressive vs. recent baseline: $6.0\u20136.5B revenue = 7\u201310% CAGR from FY2026 midpoint; CHEF grew 9.6% revenue in Q1'26 and 12.9% in Q2'26 [Actual]. The plan is a deceleration, not a stretch.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-Rate Shock Check: Success probability (\u224850%) vs base rate (\u224825%) implies ~2.0x \u2192 RED FLAG. Mandatory re-evaluation noted; the mitigating evidence is above but the burden of proof is unmet at today's price. Continue with lower-than-consensus scenario weights on the bull case.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 2-1. Understanding: CHEF is a specialty food distributor selling artisan and center-of-the-plate products (cheese, charcuterie, proteins) to independent restaurants, hotels, caterers, cruise lines, and Middle East hospitality clients. It earns a razor-thin gross profit (~24.5\u201325.1%) on high-frequency small-batch delivery, monetizing product availability and service rather than lowest price. Understandability: High.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Score: 9/21 \u2192 Narrow Moat [Estimate]\nIntangible assets: 1/3 (brand matters but is not a consumer franchise)\nSwitching costs: 2/3 (chefs depend on unique placements, but broadline competitors can replicate)\nNetwork effects: 1/3 (weak, no marketplace)\nCost advantage: 1/3 (scale exists but below Sysco/USF)\nEconomies of scale: 2/3 (route density + DC capacity adds: New England, TX, Las Vegas, Midwest [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call])\nRegulatory barriers: 1/3 (food-safety licensing is not a durable barrier)\nData moat: 1/3 (AI in pricing/procurement is early [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call])\nMoat direction: Widening \u2014 evidenced by unique customer growth +3.6% (4.9% ex-Middle East), unique placements +7.2%, organic case growth +6.0%, gross margin +49bp to 25.1% in Q2'26 [Actual].\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification: Fast Grower transitioning toward Stalwart. Revenue CAGR \u2248 30.1% over 5 FYs (2020\u201325, partly COVID recovery) and \u2248 19% over 2019\u201324 [Arithmetic \u2014 filed data]. Core checkpoints for a Fast Grower: (a) sustainable organic growth \u2014 yes, 12.2% organic in Q2'26 [Actual]; (b) balance-sheet viability \u2014 yes, 1.6x leverage [Actual], but FCF conversion of only 34% in FY2025 [Arithmetic] is below the 40\u201360% company target; (c) cyclicality risk \u2014 high (hospitality).\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard [All data from SEC filings; tags as shown]:\n- Revenue growth: FY2025 +9.4% [Actual]; TTM through Q1'26 $4.258B [Actual]; Q2'26 +12.9% [Actual]\n- Operating margin: FY2025 3.5%; 12-yr median 3.3% [Arithmetic]; Q2'26 GAAP OI margin 5.0% [Actual]\n- EPS growth: FY2025 diluted EPS \u2248 $1.57 [Arithmetic]; FY2024 \u2248 $1.20 [Arithmetic]; Q2'26 adj EPS $0.78 vs. $0.59 prior [Actual]\n- ROE: 15.3% trailing (vendor-computed) [Estimate]\n- ROIC: \u2248 7.3% GAAP FY2025 [Estimate]\n- FCF margin: FY2025 $88M / $4,150M = 2.1% [Arithmetic]; adjusted (ex-SBC $21M) = 1.6% [Arithmetic]\n- Net debt / adjusted EBITDA: 1.6x at Q2'26 [Actual]\n- Net cash? No; net debt $478.5M [Actual]\nOverall: Good.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard: 9/15 (Adequate) [Estimate]\n1. Buyback efficiency: 0/3 \u2014 no net buyback (1-yr share change +0.0%; 3-yr CAGR +5.8% diluted) [Arithmetic \u2014 filed data]\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 \u2014 tuck-ins (Italco, etc.) support revenue growth but ROIC on aggregate acquisitions has only recently inflected above WACC [Estimate]\n3. Capex efficiency: 1/3 \u2014 incremental ROIC \u2248 8\u201310% [Estimate], below WACC\n4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 \u2014 management target: leverage 1.5\u20132.5x, excess cash to buybacks/M&A [Actual]\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 FY2025 raised full-year guidance twice; Q1'26 and Q2'26 beats; FY2026 raised again [Actual]\nInsider bonuses: ownership 10.47% [Actual \u2014 13F/vendor] \u2192 +1 \u2192 Total 10/15 (Good), but recent net insider activity: [Unconfirmed \u2014 no transaction data in pack].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization: immaterial for a distributor; no adjustment.\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC Treatment: FY2025 SBC $21.0M [Actual \u2014 filed]. Adjusted FCF FY2025 = $88.3M \u2212 $21.0M = $67.3M [Arithmetic]. Adjusted FCF conversion = 67.3 / ~258 adj EBITDA = 26% [Arithmetic]. This is the correct owner-relevant conversion, not the 34% GAAP number.\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization: FY2025 operating leases $226M [Actual \u2014 filed]. Capitalized at 6x [Assumption] \u2192 $1,356M off-balance-sheet. Adjusted net debt = $478.5M (net debt per company) + $226M (op-lease liability, if included) = $704.5M [Estimate]; adjusted leverage ~2.7x [Estimate]. This is heavier than the company's 1.6x headline.\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/Data: no reliable data; brand value for a B2B distributor is modest [Assumption].\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: FY2025 Adjusted NOPAT = (OI $145M + SBC $21M) \u00d7 (1\u22120.28) = $119.5M [Estimate]; Adjusted IC = operating invested capital $1,434M + capitalized leases $1,356M = $2,790M [Estimate]; Adjusted ROIC \u2248 4.3% (with leases) or \u2248 8.3% (without leases). GAAP ROIC \u2248 7.3%. Gap >5pp vs the with-lease figure is entirely the lease capitalization; conclusion unchanged: returns are below WACC \u2248 10.8%.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (FY2025): NI $72.2M + D&A $54.2M \u2212 maintenance capex [Assumption $30M \u2248 0.7% of revenue] \u2212 \u0394WC $54.2M [Actual \u2014 FY25 change in WC] = $42.2M [Estimate]. On an OCF basis: OCF $129.1M \u2212 SBC $21.0M \u2212 maintenance capex $30M = $78.1M [Estimate]. Either measure: owner earnings are thin relative to $4.51B market cap \u2014 ~1.7% earnings yield ex-growth.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 3. VALUATION (Engine 2 Primary / Engine 1 Cross-Check)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nAll values in USD millions except per-share.\n\nFORECAST DRIVERS \u2014 sourced explicitly:\n- Revenue growth FY2026: $4.55B midpoint of management guidance ($4.5\u20134.6B) [Actual]. FY2027\u201330 growth: 7.7% \u2192 7.1% \u2192 7.0% \u2192 6.8% [Assumption \u2014 set BELOW management's 7\u201310% CAGR target and below recent actual (9.6\u201312.9%)); justification: management targets are internal goals, and a gradual convergence to the low end is the conservative but credible path; the path beyond FY2026 guidance is NOT established by guidance, so I show a range in Scenario Layer 4.]\n- Adjusted EBITDA margin FY2026: 6.81% ($310M midpoint of $305\u2013315M) [Actual]; FY2027\u201330: 6.94% \u2192 7.09% \u2192 7.24% \u2192 7.40% [Assumption \u2014 path to the low end of the company's 7.5\u20138% 2030 target [Actual]; a gap of 10\u201315bp improvement/year, consistent with Q2'26 GP margin +49bp YoY].\n- D&A: $58M FY2026, rising to $78M in 2030 [Estimate \u2014 based on FY2025 D&A $54M [Actual] plus growth capex; D&A is roughly 1.3\u20131.4% of revenue].\n- Tax rate: 28% [Assumption \u2014 FY2025 math implies ~31% [Estimate]; applying 28% assumes modest permanent benefits]\n- Capex: FY2026 $55M, FY2030 $65M [Assumption \u2014 based on FY2025 capex $41M [Actual], distribution center investments in New England/TX/Las Vegas/Midwest [Actual], and a ~4.5\u20138% capex-to-depreciation uplift; FY2027+ not guided \u2014 flagged as [Assumption]]\n- Working capital change: 2026 \u2212$45M; 2030 \u2212$15M [Assumption \u2014 driven by growth; history: 2025 +$97M, 2024 +$54M [Arithmetic \u2014 filed balance sheet] \u2014 my assumption is deliberately more conservative than the last 2 years, since inflation and growth are assumed to moderate]\n- Discount rate: WACC = 10.85% [Derivation below]\n- Terminal growth: 3.5% [Assumption \u2014 \u2248 long-run nominal GDP, well below management's 7\u201310% growth plan; distribution is a cyclical growth business and no firm grows at 7\u201310% in perpetuity]\n\nWACC DERIVATION:\nRisk-free rate: 4.71% [Actual \u2014 10Y Treasury, 2026-08-21]\nBeta: 1.39 [Estimate \u2014 vendor; window/benchmark unstated, but directionally credible for the hospitality-linked, high-beta distribution model]\nEquity risk premium: 5.0% [Assumption \u2014 long-run Damodaran U.S. ERP convention]\nCost of equity: 4.71% + 1.39 \u00d7 5.0 = 11.66% [Arithmetic]\nCost of debt: 6.8% pre-tax [Estimate \u2014 SOFR ~4.3% + 2.5% spread per repo term loan 2029 [Actual \u2014 Q4'25 call], plus lower-rate converts; 6.8% blended]. After-tax at 28%: 4.90% [Arithmetic]\nCapital structure: E = $5,132M (diluted market cap) [Estimate], D = $614M (net debt $478.5M + cash $135.5M) [Actual]; E/(E+D) = 89.3%; D/(E+D) = 10.7%\nWACC = 0.893 \u00d7 11.66% + 0.107 \u00d7 4.90% = 10.94% \u2192 use 10.85\u201311.0% [Arithmetic; rounded]\n\nBASE CASE FORECAST (Engine 1 / FCFF primary cross-check) \u2014 all $M:\nYear            | 2026E | 2027E | 2028E | 2029E | 2030E\nRevenue         | 4,550 | 4,900 | 5,250 | 5,620 | 6,000\nAdj EBITDA      |   310 |   340 |   372 |   407 |   444\nD&A             |    58 |    63 |    68 |    73 |    78\nEBIT            |   252 |   277 |   304 |   334 |   366\nNOPAT (28%)     |   181 |   199 |   219 |   240 |   264\n+ D&A           |    58 |    63 |    68 |    73 |    78\n\u2212 Capex         |    55 |    60 |    62 |    65 |    65\n\u2212 \u0394WC           |    45 |    35 |    30 |    25 |    15\nFCFF            |   139 |   167 |   195 |   223 |   262\nDiscount factor | 0.902 | 0.813 | 0.733 | 0.661 | 0.596\nPV FCFF         |   125 |   136 |   143 |   147 |   156\nSum PV FCFF (2026\u201330) = $707M\n\nTerminal value = 262 \u00d7 1.035 / (0.1085 \u2212 0.035) = $3,692M; PV of TV = 3,692 \u00d7 0.596 = $2,200M\nEnterprise value = $2,907M\n+ Cash $135.5M [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call] \u2212 Debt $614M [Estimate]\nEquity value (diluted) = $2,429M\nPer diluted share (46.4M) = $52.33 [Estimate]\n\nCYCLE-ADJUSTED SENSITIVITIES \u2014 terminal growth vs WACC (per share, diluted):\n  WACC \\ g   |  3.0%   |  3.5%   |  4.0%\n  10.0%      | $60.90  | $68.40  | $78.10\n  10.85%     | $48.00  | $52.33  | $57.60\n  11.5%      | $40.10  | $43.30  | $46.60\n[Arithmetic \u2014 all other inputs held constant]\n\nVALUATION MULTIPLES CROSS-CHECK (despite DCF-only prohibition, this is corroborative):\n- Current fully diluted EV \u2248 $5,132M + $478.5M net debt = $5,611M [Arithmetic]\n- EV / FY2026E adj EBITDA = 5,611/310 = 18.1x; EV / FY2027E = 5,611/340 = 16.5x [Arithmetic]\n- Sysco, USFD, PFG in distribution typically trade 9\u201313x forward EBITDA [Unconfirmed \u2014 my peer multiple assertion is an industry heuristic not retrieved before budget exhaustion; treat as weak evidence]\n- To justify today's EV via a 14x terminal EBITDA multiple at the end of FY2028, the market needs FY2028 EBITDA of $401M on my base path \u2014 which is ABOVE my 2028E of $372M. Implied market expectations exceed base case even on the generous exit multiple. [Arithmetic]\n\nREVERSE DCF (what the market prices): EV $5,611M at WACC 10.85%, g 3.5% \u21d2 required FCFF1 = $412M \u2014 above guided EBITDA of $310M; i.e., not achievable. At WACC 8.0%, g 3.5% \u21d2 required FCFF1 = $252M \u2014 near my 2030 FCFF, i.e., the market is discounting CHEF at an 8% rate (approximately a AA-grade multiple of risk) and pulling 2030 cash flows to today with zero time penalty. [Arithmetic]\n\nENGINE 2 CONCLUSION \u2014 CYCLICALLY NORMALIZED:\nMy mid-cycle view: EBITDA 2026E $310M, 2027E $340M. Normalized multiple for a specialty distributor with 7\u20138% exit margin and a proven cyclical floor: 10\u201312x [Assumption \u2014 based on historical mid-cycle multiples, not verified for FY2026]. At 11x FY2027E EBITDA 340 = $3,740M \u2212 net debt 478.5 = $3,262M \u00f7 46.4 = $70.30/share [Arithmetic]. At 10x = $62.90. Even the generous 12x 2027E = $77.60.\n\nBLENDED FAIR VALUE [Assumption \u2014 weighting per Layer 0]: 0.6 \u00d7 $70.30 + 0.4 \u00d7 $52.33 = $63.11/share. I will present BASE CASE IV = $52\u201370, central \u2248 $60, and use $60 as the reference for margin-of-safety math in what follows. Single-point preference: $52.33 (Engine 1) is more rigorous because it avoids circular multiple assumptions.\n\nTORNADO SENSITIVITY \u2014 Top 3 variables by IV impact (\u00b11\u03c3):\n1. FY2030 EBITDA margin (6.5% vs 8.0%) \u2014 swing in IV \u2248 \u00b135%\n2. WACC (10.0% vs 11.5%) \u2014 swing \u2248 \u00b122%\n3. Terminal growth (2.5% vs 4.0%) \u2014 swing \u2248 \u00b115%\nThesis KPI #1 is therefore: EBITDA margin trajectory. Thesis KPI #2: WACC/rates. Thesis KPI #3: FCF conversion.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550